into the night a fiery spout of lava and  clouds of dark smoke spew out of the  volcano in the commbre vieja national  park  the eruption began with a thunderous  roar earlier in the day and spain's  canary islands off the coast of  northwestern africa the island of la  palma had already been on high alert  after more than 22 000 tremors were  reported in the space of a week  but just days ago scientists had said  they didn't believe an eruption was  imminent  i don't know how to explain it it was  powerful powerful to see a sudden  explosion like that  we were on orange alert there were loads  of tremors but being on orange alert i  couldn't imagine it would all be so  sudden  the molten liquids shooting hundreds of  meters into the sky and pouring down the  hillside closer to some isolated  villages  thousands of people are being moved from  their homes but it's thought most of the  island's 85 000 residents are not at  risk  i'm very scared we didn't expect it we  were warned but we didn't expect it  we've had earthquakes all night this  morning i had to hold on to the tv  in the last major eruption there in 1971  one man was killed as he took  photographs near the lava flows  authorities are warning the curious not  to get too close and stay inside if  there's volcanic ash nearby  spanish prime minister pedro sanchez has  put off his trip to new york to attend  the un general assembly instead  traveling to the canary islands to meet  with local officials  right now we are keeping a close eye on  the fires that may happen as a result of  the eruption we will deploy not only the  civil guard who are already there but  also the national police forest  firefighters and members of the military  emergencies unit  the kumbra vieja is one of the most  active volcanic regions in the canaries  volcanologists say while it's too early  to know for sure this eruption could  last for weeks or even months alex  o'brien al jazeera
 

